//! signal — signals delivered (px26): the calls that set actions and
//! masks and send signals, the frame a handler runs on, `rt_sigreturn`,
//! the default actions, and what a signal does to a system call that
//! was waiting.
//!
//! Written from man-pages `signal(7)`, `sigaction(2)`, `sigprocmask(2)`,
//! `sigreturn(2)`, `sigpending(2)`, `kill(2)`, `tgkill(2)`,
//! `sigaltstack(2)`, `pipe(7)`; the uapi headers
//! `arch/x86/include/uapi/asm/signal.h` (the numbers, SA_*),
//! `asm/sigcontext.h` (`struct sigcontext_64`, the extended state's
//! software bytes and the two magics), `asm/ucontext.h` (UC_FP_XSTATE,
//! UC_SIGCONTEXT_SS, UC_STRICT_RESTORE_SS), `asm-generic/ucontext.h`
//! and `asm-generic/siginfo.h` (siginfo's layout, SI_*, CLD_*, SEGV_*,
//! ILL_*, FPE_*); the x86-64 psABI (the red zone, the stack's alignment
//! at a function's entry); the Intel SDM vol. 1 §13 (XSAVE's area); and
//! black-box runs on Linux of user/elf/threads.c, which prints what a
//! handler is given (`notes/px26/`). No Linux source; no other
//! kernel's source; no libc's.
//!
//! **When a signal is taken**: at a thread's return to ring 3, and only
//! there. boot/isr.S's common path calls kernel/interrupts, which hands
//! the frame it is about to resume to `on_return`: while the running
//! thread has a pending signal it does not block (kernel/sched keeps
//! the sets and picks the thread: a process's signal goes to its leader
//! when the leader has it unblocked, else to its lowest-numbered thread
//! that has), the lowest-numbered is taken and acted on. So a signal a
//! thread sends itself is delivered before `kill` returns to it, and
//! all that are pending are taken before one instruction of the
//! program runs: each handler's frame is laid on top of the last, so
//! the highest-numbered runs first (measured on Linux: 15, 12, 10).
//! A thread waiting in a system call is made ready by kernel/sched when
//! a signal arrives that will run a handler or end its process.
//!
//! **What a taken signal does** (`act`):
//! - SIG_IGN, or the default for SIGCHLD, SIGURG, SIGWINCH and SIGCONT:
//!   nothing.
//! - the default for SIGSTOP, SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN, SIGTTOU (px48): the
//!   process stops — one that is sent while some thread takes it stops
//!   the process when it is sent (kernel/sched's `job_signal`), one that
//!   was blocked when it is taken here — every thread parked until a
//!   SIGCONT (or SIGKILL); its parent is told (SIGCHLD with CLD_STOPPED
//!   unless SA_NOCLDSTOP, and `wait4`'s WUNTRACED). SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN and
//!   SIGTTOU to a process whose group is orphaned are discarded (XBD
//!   11.1.4). A parked thread is parked here, on its way to ring 3.
//! - any default for pid 1: nothing (`kill(2)`: init gets only the
//!   signals it has a handler for).
//! - any other default: the process ends, every thread of it, its wait
//!   status the signal's number (no core is written, so bit 7 is
//!   clear): `user: <name> pid <n> killed: SIG<NAME>[, a write to a
//!   pipe with no reader] after <r> runs`.
//! - a handler: the frame below, and the thread resumes in the handler.
//!
//! **The frame**, on the thread's own stack below the 128-byte red
//! zone — or, for a handler installed with SA_ONSTACK by a thread that
//! has an alternate stack and is not on it, from that stack's top
//! (px24: `sigaltstack`) — from the top down:
//! - the extended state, 64-aligned: what XSAVE writes for the features
//!   XCR0 enables (the FXSAVE image, the header, AVX's part), with the
//!   software bytes at 464 (magic 0x46505853, the size with its closing
//!   word, XCR0's features, the size) and the word 0x46505845 after it;
//!   on a CPU without XSAVE, FXSAVE's 512 bytes and no magic;
//! - the siginfo, 128 bytes: si_signo, si_errno 0, si_code, then by
//!   kind the sender's pid and real user id (`kill`, `tgkill`, a pipe;
//!   px45, measured: the real id, not the effective one), the child's
//!   pid, real user id and status (SIGCHLD), or the address (a fault);
//! - the ucontext, 304 bytes: uc_flags (7 with XSAVE: the three UC_*
//!   bits; 6 without), uc_link 0, a zero stack triple, the sigcontext
//!   (r8-r15, rdi, rsi, rbp, rbx, rdx, rax, rcx, rsp, rip, eflags, cs
//!   and ss, err, trapno, the old mask, cr2, the extended state's
//!   address), then the old mask again;
//! - the restorer's address (SA_RESTORER's, which every libc gives),
//!   where the handler's `ret` finds it, placed so RSP + 8 is a
//!   multiple of 16.
//! The handler starts with RDI the number, RSI the siginfo, RDX the
//! ucontext, RAX 0, DF and TF clear, and the x87/SSE state fresh (FCW
//! 0x037f, MXCSR 0x1f80: measured on Linux); its mask is the old one
//! with `sa_mask` and, without SA_NODEFER, the signal itself; under
//! SA_RESETHAND the action's handler is the default again (its flags
//! stay: measured). `rt_sigreturn` (15) finds the ucontext at RSP,
//! puts back every general register, RIP, RSP, the flags a program may
//! set, the mask and the extended state (made loadable first:
//! kernel/fpu's `adopt`), and leaves by IRETQ so RCX and R11 come back
//! too. A frame the stack has no room for, a ucontext that cannot be
//! read, a RIP outside the user half or an extended state XRSTOR would
//! refuse ends the process with SIGSEGV.
//!
//! **Where PAX's frame differs from Linux's, by name**: the selectors
//! in the sigcontext are PAX's (cs 0x23, ss 0x1b; Linux's are 0x33 and
//! 0x2b), ring 3 both; `rt_sigreturn` ignores what a handler wrote
//! there.
//!
//! **A system call that was waiting** (kernel/sched's W_*): when a
//! handler is about to run and the thread was made ready out of a
//! wait, the saved context is that of the call, to be run again or to
//! answer -EINTR (`signal(7)`, measured with user/elf/threads.c):
//! - a read of the console, a pipe or a socketpair, a pipe write,
//!   `wait4`, `waitid`: run again under SA_RESTART, else -EINTR;
//! - a futex wait with no timeout: the same;
//! - `nanosleep`, `clock_nanosleep`: -EINTR always, the time left
//!   written for a relative sleep;
//! - a futex wait with a timeout, a receive with one: -EINTR always.
//! A signal that does nothing wakes nobody; one that ends the process
//! needs no answer.
//!
//! **Sending**: `kill` (62) — a pid is that process; 0 the caller's
//! process group, a negative pid the group it names (px48: every
//! process of it not yet reaped, a zombie included, -ESRCH when it has
//! none); -1 is every process but pid 1 and the caller; signal 0 only
//! asks; -ESRCH, -EINVAL. `tgkill` (234) and `tkill` (200): that thread,
//! -ESRCH when it has ended. The terminal's signals (`tty`): the
//! console's foreground process group (px48; every process before).
//! Real-time signals (32-64) are signals like the rest: one instance
//! pending, not a queue (named).
//!
//! **Who may signal whom** (px45; `kill(2)`, measured on Linux 7.2.8 as
//! real root: px38's `sig`, 102 rows, and px45's `rights`): `may_signal`,
//! the one rule. A sender may signal a target when it is privileged
//! (effective user id 0, kernel/cred's `privileged`: ruling #75 R9), or
//! its real or effective user id is the target's real or saved user id
//! — a target's effective id is never asked, and a sender whose only
//! match is its own saved id is refused — or the signal is SIGCONT and
//! both are in one session (px48: their sessions, measured: SIGCONT to
//! root's process in another session is -EPERM to a user). Else -EPERM.
//! Asked by `kill` of a pid, by `tgkill` and `tkill` of the thread's
//! process, and of each process `kill(-1)`, `kill(0)` and a negative
//! group reach (each one signalled only when the rule allows: `kill(-1)`
//! answers 0 whenever some other process than pid 1 and the caller
//! exists, permitted or not, and a group 0 when at least one member was
//! permitted, a zombie too, measured). A zombie is a
//! process until it is reaped: `kill`, `tgkill` and `tkill` of one
//! answer by the rule, against the ids it ended with, and signal
//! nothing (measured; -ESRCH before px45). Signal 0 asks the same. What
//! the kernel raises asks nothing: a child's end (its parent gets
//! SIGCHLD whatever either's ids, measured), the terminal's keys, a
//! write to a pipe with no reader, a fault. Validity is answered first
//! (-EINVAL for a bad signal, then -ESRCH for no such process or a
//! thread not in its group), the rule last.
//!
//! The actions are kernel/process's words in the process area; masks
//! and pending sets are kernel/sched's. No region, no heap: `act` may
//! end the thread.

use cred
use files
use fpu
use frames
use log
use netd
use paging
use process
use sched
use timer
use uaccess
use vm

extern "c" fn pax_cr2() -> u64

const USER_CS: u64 = 0x23
const USER_SS: u64 = 0x1b

// boot/isr.S's frame, as word indices.
const W_R15: u64 = 0
const W_R14: u64 = 1
const W_R13: u64 = 2
const W_R12: u64 = 3
const W_R11: u64 = 4
const W_R10: u64 = 5
const W_R9: u64 = 6
const W_R8: u64 = 7
const W_RBP: u64 = 8
const W_RDI: u64 = 9
const W_RSI: u64 = 10
const W_RDX: u64 = 11
const W_RCX: u64 = 12
const W_RBX: u64 = 13
const W_RAX: u64 = 14
const W_VECTOR: u64 = 15
const W_ERROR: u64 = 16
const W_RIP: u64 = 17
const W_CS: u64 = 18
const W_RFLAGS: u64 = 19
const W_RSP: u64 = 20
const W_SS: u64 = 21
// A vector no gate has and boot/isr.S does not take for SYSRETQ's: a
// frame a signal rewrote leaves by IRETQ, which restores RCX and R11.
const SIGNAL_VECTOR: u64 = 0x103

// The area's signal actions (kernel/process): four words a signal.
const SIG_WORDS: u64 = 256
const SIG_DFL: u64 = 0
const SIG_IGN: u64 = 1
const NSIG: u64 = 64
const UNBLOCKABLE: u64 = 0x40100 // SIGKILL's bit (8) and SIGSTOP's (18)

// uapi asm/signal.h.
const SA_SIGINFO: u64 = 0x4
const SA_RESTORER: u64 = 0x04000000
const SA_ONSTACK: u64 = 0x08000000 // px24
const SS_ONSTACK: u64 = 1 // px24: uapi asm-generic/signal-defs.h
const SS_DISABLE: u64 = 2
const MINSIGSTKSZ: u64 = 2048 // px24: uapi asm/signal.h
const A_REFUSAL: u64 = 3 // px24: the area's refusal word
/// px24: the refusal code kernel/ready names: a `sigaltstack` flag PAX
/// does not take (its range, 96 and up).
pub const R_ALTSTACK: u64 = 104
const NEG_ENOMEM: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff4 // -12
const SA_NODEFER: u64 = 0x40000000
const SA_RESETHAND: u64 = 0x80000000
const SA_RESTART: u64 = 0x10000000
const SIG_BLOCK: u64 = 0
const SIG_UNBLOCK: u64 = 1
const SIG_SETMASK: u64 = 2
const SIGILL: u64 = 4
const SIGTRAP: u64 = 5
const SIGBUS: u64 = 7
const SIGFPE: u64 = 8
const SIGKILL: u64 = 9
const SIGSEGV: u64 = 11
const SIGPIPE: u64 = 13
const SIGCHLD: u64 = 17
const SIGCONT: u64 = 18
const SIGSTOP: u64 = 19
const SIGTSTP: u64 = 20
const SIGTTIN: u64 = 21
const SIGTTOU: u64 = 22
const SIGURG: u64 = 23
const SIGWINCH: u64 = 28

// siginfo's codes (uapi asm-generic/siginfo.h).
const SI_USER: u64 = 0
const SI_KERNEL: u64 = 0x80
const SI_TKILL: u64 = 0xfffffffa // -6 as an int
const CLD_EXITED: u64 = 1
const CLD_KILLED: u64 = 2
const CLD_STOPPED: u64 = 5 // px48
const CLD_CONTINUED: u64 = 6
const SEGV_MAPERR: u64 = 1
const SEGV_ACCERR: u64 = 2
const BUS_ADRERR: u64 = 2 // px62: a mapping's page past its file's end (siginfo.h)
const ILL_ILLOPN: u64 = 2
const FPE_INTDIV: u64 = 1
const FPE_FLTINV: u64 = 7

// The frame's pieces.
const RED_ZONE: u64 = 128
const SIGINFO_BYTES: u64 = 128
const UC_BYTES: u64 = 304
const UC_FLAGS_XSTATE: u64 = 0x7 // UC_FP_XSTATE | UC_SIGCONTEXT_SS | UC_STRICT_RESTORE_SS
const UC_FLAGS_PLAIN: u64 = 0x6
const UC_MCONTEXT: u64 = 40
const UC_SIGMASK: u64 = 296
const MAGIC1: u64 = 0x46505853
const MAGIC2: u64 = 0x46505845
// RFLAGS bits a program may set through a saved context: CF PF AF ZF
// SF TF DF OF, RF, AC, ID. IF stays set, IOPL 0.
const FLAGS_USER: u64 = 0x250dd5
const FLAGS_FIXED: u64 = 0x202
const FLAG_TF: u64 = 0x100
const FLAG_DF: u64 = 0x400

// -errno as %rax carries it.
const NEG_EPERM: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffff // -1
const NEG_ESRCH: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffffd // -3
const NEG_EINTR: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffffc // -4
const NEG_EFAULT: u64 = 0xfffffffffffffff2 // -14
const NEG_EINVAL: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffea // -22

const NS_PER_SEC: u64 = 1000000000
const NS_PER_TICK: u64 = 10000151 // kernel/user's

fn word(f: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: `f` is the trampoline's frame (22 words), live for the
    // handler that passed it; i < 22.
    unsafe { ((f + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put_word(f: u64, i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `word`.
    unsafe { ((f + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn area() -> u64 {
    sched.proc_area(sched.self_slot())
}

fn aget(a: u64, i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: a process area (sched.proc_area), 512 words; i < 512.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn aput(a: u64, i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `aget`.
    unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn bit(sig: u64) -> u64 {
    1 << (sig - 1)
}

/// A signal's name, for the lines.
fn name(sig: u64) -> str {
    match sig {
        1 => "SIGHUP",
        2 => "SIGINT",
        3 => "SIGQUIT",
        4 => "SIGILL",
        5 => "SIGTRAP",
        6 => "SIGABRT",
        7 => "SIGBUS",
        8 => "SIGFPE",
        9 => "SIGKILL",
        10 => "SIGUSR1",
        11 => "SIGSEGV",
        12 => "SIGUSR2",
        13 => "SIGPIPE",
        14 => "SIGALRM",
        15 => "SIGTERM",
        16 => "SIGSTKFLT",
        17 => "SIGCHLD",
        18 => "SIGCONT",
        19 => "SIGSTOP",
        20 => "SIGTSTP",
        21 => "SIGTTIN",
        22 => "SIGTTOU",
        23 => "SIGURG",
        24 => "SIGXCPU",
        25 => "SIGXFSZ",
        26 => "SIGVTALRM",
        27 => "SIGPROF",
        28 => "SIGWINCH",
        29 => "SIGIO",
        30 => "SIGPWR",
        31 => "SIGSYS",
        _ => "a real-time signal",
    }
}

// --- the calls ------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `rt_sigprocmask(how, set, oldset, sigsetsize)`: the running thread's
/// mask (px14 kept one a process; a thread has its own).
pub fn sigprocmask(how: u64, set: u64, oldset: u64, size: u64) -> u64 {
    if size != 8 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    let old = sched.self_sigmask()
    var m = old
    if set != 0 {
        if !uaccess.readable(pml4, set, 8) {
            return NEG_EFAULT
        }
        let v = uaccess.get64(pml4, set)
        let h = how & 0xffffffff
        if h == SIG_BLOCK {
            m = m | v
        } else if h == SIG_UNBLOCK {
            m = m & !v
        } else if h == SIG_SETMASK {
            m = v
        } else {
            return NEG_EINVAL
        }
        m = m & !UNBLOCKABLE
    }
    if oldset != 0 {
        if !uaccess.writable(pml4, oldset, 8) {
            return NEG_EFAULT
        }
        uaccess.put(pml4, oldset, old, 8)
    }
    if m != old {
        sched.set_self_sigmask(m)
        sched.masks_changed()
    }
    0
}

/// `rt_sigaction(sig, act, oldact, sigsetsize)`: the kernel's `struct
/// sigaction` on x86-64 is four words (handler, flags, restorer, mask).
/// SIGKILL's and SIGSTOP's cannot be set (-EINVAL) and can be read.
pub fn sigaction(sig: u64, act: u64, oldact: u64, size: u64) -> u64 {
    let n = sig & 0xffffffff
    if size != 8 || n < 1 || n > NSIG || (act != 0 && (n == SIGKILL || n == SIGSTOP)) {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    let a = area()
    let w = SIG_WORDS + (n - 1) * 4
    if act != 0 && !uaccess.readable(pml4, act, 32) {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    if oldact != 0 {
        if !uaccess.writable(pml4, oldact, 32) {
            return NEG_EFAULT
        }
        var i: u64 = 0
        while i < 4 {
            uaccess.put(pml4, oldact + i * 8, aget(a, w + i), 8)
            i += 1
        }
    }
    if act != 0 {
        var i: u64 = 0
        while i < 4 {
            aput(a, w + i, uaccess.get64(pml4, act + i * 8))
            i += 1
        }
        aput(a, w + 3, aget(a, w + 3) & !UNBLOCKABLE)
        sched.masks_changed() // a pending signal's action may now wake a thread
    }
    0
}

/// `rt_sigpending(set, sigsetsize)`: what is pending for the thread and
/// for its process and blocked by the thread.
pub fn sigpending(set: u64, size: u64) -> u64 {
    if size != 8 {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    if !uaccess.writable(pml4, set, 8) {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    uaccess.put(pml4, set, sched.self_pending() & sched.self_sigmask(), 8)
    0
}

/// A 32-bit argument read as an int, sign and magnitude.
fn int_of(x: u64) -> (bool, u64) {
    let v = x & 0xffffffff
    if v >= 0x80000000 {
        return (true, 0x100000000 - v)
    }
    (false, v)
}

/// px45: THE rule of who may signal whom (`kill(2)`; the module's
/// comment): may the process whose area is `me` send `sig` to the
/// process whose area is `t`?
pub fn may_signal(me: u64, t: u64, sig: u64) -> bool {
    if cred.privileged(me) {
        return true
    }
    if sig == SIGCONT && sched.sid_of_area(me) == sched.sid_of_area(t) {
        return true // px48: within a session, anyone's
    }
    let r = cred.uid(me, cred.REAL)
    let e = cred.uid(me, cred.EFFECTIVE)
    let tr = cred.uid(t, cred.REAL)
    let ts = cred.uid(t, cred.SAVED)
    r == tr || r == ts || e == tr || e == ts
}

/// px45: the area of process `pid` (a zombie's too: its ids are kept
/// until it is reaped), or 0 when there is no such process.
fn area_of_pid(pid: u64) -> u64 {
    let sl = sched.proc_slot(pid)
    if sl == sched.SLOTS {
        return 0
    }
    sched.proc_area_of_leader(sl)
}

/// px45: whether process `pid` has ended and is not yet reaped.
fn is_zombie(pid: u64) -> bool {
    let sl = sched.proc_slot(pid)
    sl != sched.SLOTS && sched.proc_word(sl, 8) == 1
}

/// `kill(pid, sig)`.
pub fn kill(pid: u64, sig: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = sig & 0xffffffff
    if s > NSIG {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    let me = sched.self_pid()
    let a = area()
    let info = sched.sig_info(sched.I_USER, me, 0, cred.uid(a, cred.REAL))
    let (neg, p) = int_of(pid)
    if !neg && p != 0 {
        let t = area_of_pid(p)
        if t == 0 {
            return NEG_ESRCH
        }
        if !may_signal(a, t, s) {
            return NEG_EPERM
        }
        let _live = sched.raise_pid(p, s, info) // a zombie takes nothing
        return 0
    }
    // -1: every process but pid 1 and the caller (`kill(2)`), each by
    // the rule.
    if neg && p == 1 {
        var seen: u64 = 0
        var q = sched.proc_after(0)
        while q != 0 {
            if q != 1 && q != me {
                seen += 1
                let t = area_of_pid(q)
                if t != 0 && may_signal(a, t, s) {
                    let _live = sched.raise_pid(q, s, info)
                }
            }
            q = sched.proc_after(q)
        }
        return if seen > 0 { 0 } else { NEG_ESRCH } // measured: 0 with none permitted
    }
    // px48: 0 is the caller's process group, -g group g: every member not
    // yet reaped (a zombie is one, and is signalled nothing), each by the
    // rule; -ESRCH with no member, 0 when some member was permitted,
    // else -EPERM (measured, s240's grp and this lane's jobs).
    let g = if p == 0 { sched.self_pgid() } else { p }
    var seen: u64 = 0
    var sent: u64 = 0
    var q = sched.member_after(g, 0)
    while q != 0 {
        seen += 1
        let t = area_of_pid(q)
        if t != 0 && may_signal(a, t, s) {
            sent += 1
            let _live = sched.raise_pid(q, s, info)
        }
        q = sched.member_after(g, q)
    }
    if seen == 0 {
        return NEG_ESRCH
    }
    if sent > 0 { 0 } else { NEG_EPERM }
}

/// `tgkill(tgid, tid, sig)` and `tkill(tid, sig)` (`tgid` 0).
pub fn tgkill(tgid: u64, tid: u64, sig: u64, any_group: bool) -> u64 {
    let s = sig & 0xffffffff
    let (gneg, g) = int_of(tgid)
    let (tneg, t) = int_of(tid)
    if s > NSIG || tneg || t == 0 || (!any_group && (gneg || g == 0)) {
        return NEG_EINVAL
    }
    // px45: the process the thread is in (for `tkill`, whichever holds
    // it); a zombie's leader is its own thread until it is reaped.
    var owner: u64 = 0
    if any_group {
        var q = sched.proc_after(0)
        while q != 0 && owner == 0 {
            if sched.raise_tid(q, t, 0, 0) || (q == t && is_zombie(q)) {
                owner = q
            }
            q = sched.proc_after(q)
        }
    } else if sched.raise_tid(g, t, 0, 0) || (g == t && is_zombie(g)) {
        owner = g
    }
    if owner == 0 {
        return NEG_ESRCH
    }
    let a = area()
    let ta = area_of_pid(owner)
    if ta == 0 || !may_signal(a, ta, s) {
        return NEG_EPERM
    }
    if s != 0 {
        let _live = sched.raise_tid(owner, t, s, sched.sig_info(sched.I_TKILL, sched.self_pid(), 0, cred.uid(a, cred.REAL)))
    }
    0
}

/// A write met a pipe with no reader, or (`conn`, px23) a stream socket
/// whose connection is over (kernel/user, before it answers -EPIPE):
/// SIGPIPE for the writing thread, from itself (`pipe(7)`; siginfo's
/// SI_USER with its own pid, measured).
pub fn pipe(conn: bool) {
    sched.raise_thread(sched.self_slot(), SIGPIPE, sched.sig_info(if conn { sched.I_CONN } else { sched.I_PIPE }, sched.self_pid(), 0, cred.uid(area(), cred.REAL)))
}

/// The terminal's signals (kernel/console's ISIG; `mask` has bit n - 1
/// for each signal a key raised): px48: for the console's foreground
/// process group only (XBD 11.1.9), nobody when no session holds the
/// console. Called from the console's interrupt, IF clear.
pub fn tty(mask: u64) {
    let g = sched.tty_pgrp()
    if g == 0 || sched.tty_session() == 0 {
        return
    }
    var sig: u64 = 1
    while sig <= 32 {
        if mask & bit(sig) != 0 {
            let _n = sched.raise_pgrp(g, sig, sched.sig_info(sched.I_KERNEL, 0, 0, 0))
        }
        sig += 1
    }
}

// --- delivery ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `user: <name> pid <n>` for the running thread (kernel/process's).
fn who() {
    process.who()
}

/// The running thread's process ends by signal `sig`: the line, its
/// descriptors closed, every thread ended; the next thread's frame.
fn die(f: u64, sig: u64, why: str) -> u64 {
    who()
    log.put(" killed: ")
    log.put(name(sig))
    log.put(why)
    log.put(" after ")
    log.dec(sched.self_runs() as int)
    log.put(" runs")
    log.end()
    files.close_all(area())
    let _n = netd.drain() // px23: what its sockets' ends sent to this machine
    sched.end_with(f, sig)
}

/// The siginfo at user `si` for signal `sig` with origin word `info`
/// (kernel/sched's `sig_info`); `addr` is a fault's address. px45:
/// `si_uid` (at 20) the sender's, or the child's, real user id.
fn put_siginfo(pml4: u64, si: u64, sig: u64, code: u64, pid: u64, uid: u64, status: u64, addr: u64, fault: bool) {
    uaccess.zero(pml4, si, SIGINFO_BYTES)
    uaccess.put(pml4, si, sig, 4)
    uaccess.put(pml4, si + 8, code, 4)
    if fault {
        uaccess.put(pml4, si + 16, addr, 8)
    } else {
        uaccess.put(pml4, si + 16, pid, 4)
        uaccess.put(pml4, si + 20, uid, 4)
        uaccess.put(pml4, si + 24, status, 4)
    }
}

/// When the thread came out of a waiting system call (kernel/sched's
/// `self_wait`), make the frame `f` the context a handler returns to:
/// the call again, or its -EINTR. `restart`: the action has SA_RESTART.
fn settle_wait(f: u64, restart: bool) {
    files.fifo_abandon() // px62: a FIFO's waiting open gives its count back (run again, it counts afresh)
    let (w, arg) = sched.self_wait()
    if w == sched.W_NONE || w == sched.W_SUSPEND {
        return // px48: rt_sigsuspend's -EINTR is in the frame already
    }
    if w == sched.W_REWOUND {
        // The frame is rewound to the `syscall` (two bytes), %rax its number.
        if !restart {
            put_word(f, W_RIP, word(f, W_RIP) + 2)
            put_word(f, W_RAX, NEG_EINTR)
        }
        return
    }
    if w == sched.W_TIMED_REWOUND {
        put_word(f, W_RIP, word(f, W_RIP) + 2)
        put_word(f, W_RAX, NEG_EINTR)
        return
    }
    if w == sched.W_TIMED {
        put_word(f, W_RAX, NEG_EINTR) // over the -ETIMEDOUT the wait left
        return
    }
    if w == sched.W_FUTEX {
        if restart {
            put_word(f, W_RIP, word(f, W_RIP) - 2)
            put_word(f, W_RAX, 202)
        } else {
            put_word(f, W_RAX, NEG_EINTR)
        }
        return
    }
    if w == sched.W_SLEEP {
        put_word(f, W_RAX, NEG_EINTR)
        // `arg`: where a relative sleep's time left goes, and the tick it
        // would have ended at, in kernel/sched's wake word (still there).
        if arg != 0 {
            let pml4 = paging.live()
            if uaccess.writable(pml4, arg, 16) {
                let end = sched.self_wake()
                let now = timer.ticks()
                let left = if end > now { (end - now) * NS_PER_TICK } else { 0 }
                uaccess.put(pml4, arg, left / NS_PER_SEC, 8)
                uaccess.put(pml4, arg + 8, left % NS_PER_SEC, 8)
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Lay signal `sig`'s frame on the user stack and point `f` at the
/// handler. False when the stack has no room (nothing changed).
/// `fault`: siginfo carries `addr`, and the sigcontext `trapno`, `err`
/// and `cr2`.
fn enter(f: u64, sig: u64, code: u64, pid: u64, uid: u64, status: u64, fault: bool, addr: u64, trapno: u64, err: u64, saved: u64) -> bool {
    let a = area()
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    let w = SIG_WORDS + (sig - 1) * 4
    let handler = aget(a, w)
    let flags = aget(a, w + 1)
    let restorer = aget(a, w + 2)
    let amask = aget(a, w + 3)
    let s = sched.self_slot()
    let fb = fpu.frame_bytes()
    let xs = fpu.xsave()
    // Top down: the extended state (and its closing word), 64-aligned;
    // then siginfo, ucontext and the return address, with (rsp + 8) % 16
    // == 0 at the handler's first instruction.
    var sp = word(f, W_RSP) - RED_ZONE
    // px24: a handler installed with SA_ONSTACK runs on the thread's
    // alternate stack, from its top, unless the thread is on it already
    // (sigaltstack(2)).
    let (asp, asz) = sched.self_altstack()
    let on_alt = asz != 0 && word(f, W_RSP) > asp && word(f, W_RSP) - asp <= asz
    if flags & SA_ONSTACK != 0 && asz != 0 && !on_alt {
        sp = asp + asz
    }
    var fp: u64 = 0
    if fb != 0 {
        if sp < fb + 8 + 64 {
            return false
        }
        fp = (sp - fb - 8) & !63
        sp = fp
    }
    if sp < SIGINFO_BYTES + UC_BYTES + 64 {
        return false
    }
    let fr = ((sp - SIGINFO_BYTES - UC_BYTES - 8) & !15) - 8
    let uc = fr + 8
    let si = uc + UC_BYTES
    let top = if fb != 0 { fp + fb + 8 } else { si + SIGINFO_BYTES }
    if !uaccess.writable(pml4, fr, top - fr) {
        return false
    }
    // The extended state: the live registers, as XSAVE writes them.
    if fb != 0 {
        let src = fpu.capture(s)
        let _ok = uaccess.copy_out(pml4, fp, src, fb)
        if xs {
            uaccess.zero(pml4, fp + 464, 48)
            uaccess.put(pml4, fp + 464, MAGIC1, 4)
            uaccess.put(pml4, fp + 468, fb + 4, 4)
            uaccess.put(pml4, fp + 472, fpu.features(), 8)
            uaccess.put(pml4, fp + 480, fb, 4)
            uaccess.put(pml4, fp + fb, MAGIC2, 4)
        }
    }
    put_siginfo(pml4, si, sig, code, pid, uid, status, addr, fault)
    // px48: `saved` is the mask the frame gives back at `rt_sigreturn`
    // (the thread's own, or the one `rt_sigsuspend` replaced); the
    // handler runs with the thread's mask now and the action's.
    let old = saved
    let now = sched.self_sigmask()
    uaccess.zero(pml4, uc, UC_BYTES)
    uaccess.put(pml4, uc, if xs { UC_FLAGS_XSTATE } else { UC_FLAGS_PLAIN }, 8)
    if asz != 0 {
        // px24: uc_stack, the alternate stack as the interrupted code had it.
        uaccess.put(pml4, uc + 16, asp, 8)
        uaccess.put(pml4, uc + 24, if on_alt { SS_ONSTACK } else { 0 }, 4)
        uaccess.put(pml4, uc + 32, asz, 8)
    }
    let m = uc + UC_MCONTEXT
    uaccess.put(pml4, m, word(f, W_R8), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 8, word(f, W_R9), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 16, word(f, W_R10), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 24, word(f, W_R11), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 32, word(f, W_R12), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 40, word(f, W_R13), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 48, word(f, W_R14), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 56, word(f, W_R15), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 64, word(f, W_RDI), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 72, word(f, W_RSI), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 80, word(f, W_RBP), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 88, word(f, W_RBX), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 96, word(f, W_RDX), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 104, word(f, W_RAX), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 112, word(f, W_RCX), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 120, word(f, W_RSP), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 128, word(f, W_RIP), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 136, word(f, W_RFLAGS), 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 144, USER_CS | (USER_SS << 48), 8) // cs, gs, fs, ss
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 152, err, 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 160, trapno, 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 168, old, 8) // oldmask
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 176, if fault { addr } else { 0 }, 8) // cr2
    uaccess.put(pml4, m + 184, fp, 8) // fpstate
    uaccess.put(pml4, uc + UC_SIGMASK, old, 8)
    uaccess.put(pml4, fr, restorer, 8)
    // The handler's first instruction.
    var nm = now | amask
    if flags & SA_NODEFER == 0 {
        nm = nm | bit(sig)
    }
    sched.set_self_sigmask(nm & !UNBLOCKABLE)
    if flags & SA_RESETHAND != 0 {
        aput(a, w, SIG_DFL) // the handler alone: the flags stay (measured)
    }
    fpu.exec(s) // a fresh x87/SSE state (measured: FCW 0x037f, MXCSR 0x1f80)
    put_word(f, W_RIP, handler)
    put_word(f, W_RSP, fr)
    put_word(f, W_RDI, sig)
    put_word(f, W_RSI, si)
    put_word(f, W_RDX, uc)
    put_word(f, W_RAX, 0)
    put_word(f, W_RFLAGS, (word(f, W_RFLAGS) & !(FLAG_TF | FLAG_DF)) | FLAGS_FIXED)
    put_word(f, W_CS, USER_CS)
    put_word(f, W_SS, USER_SS)
    put_word(f, W_VECTOR, SIGNAL_VECTOR)
    let n = sched.count_signal()
    if n % 2500 == 0 && !log.quiet() {
        // The census line (tests/mpx3-threads' soak reads four of them).
        who()
        log.put(" signals: ")
        log.dec(n as int)
        log.put(" delivered since boot; slots busy ")
        log.dec(sched.slots_busy() as int)
        log.put(", frames free ")
        log.dec(frames.free_count() as int)
        log.put(", this frame at ")
        log.hex(fr)
        log.end()
    }
    sched.masks_changed()
    true
}

/// px24: `sigaltstack(ss, old_ss)` from the frame `f` (`sigaltstack(2)`;
/// wolf's hosted runtime gives every thread one, for a handler that
/// must run when the thread's own stack has overflowed): the thread's
/// alternate signal stack read into `old_ss` (`ss_flags` SS_DISABLE
/// when it has none, SS_ONSTACK while it runs on it) and, with `ss`,
/// set: SS_DISABLE takes it away; any other flag is -EINVAL
/// (SS_AUTODISARM by name: nothing asks for it); a stack under
/// MINSIGSTKSZ bytes is -ENOMEM; a change while the thread runs on it
/// is -EPERM. A thread starts with none, a fork's child keeps its
/// parent's, `execve` drops it.
pub fn sigaltstack(f: u64, ss: u64, old: u64) -> u64 {
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    let (asp, asz) = sched.self_altstack()
    let rsp = word(f, W_RSP)
    let on = asz != 0 && rsp > asp && rsp - asp <= asz
    if old != 0 && !uaccess.writable(pml4, old, 24) {
        return NEG_EFAULT
    }
    var nsp = asp
    var nsz = asz
    if ss != 0 {
        if !uaccess.readable(pml4, ss, 24) {
            return NEG_EFAULT
        }
        if on {
            return NEG_EPERM
        }
        let fl = uaccess.get(pml4, ss + 8, 4)
        if fl & !SS_DISABLE != 0 {
            if fl == 0x80000000 || fl == 0x80000002 {
                aput(area(), A_REFUSAL, R_ALTSTACK)
            }
            return NEG_EINVAL
        }
        if fl & SS_DISABLE != 0 {
            nsp = 0
            nsz = 0
        } else {
            nsz = uaccess.get64(pml4, ss + 16)
            if nsz < MINSIGSTKSZ {
                return NEG_ENOMEM
            }
            nsp = uaccess.get64(pml4, ss)
        }
    }
    if old != 0 {
        uaccess.zero(pml4, old, 24)
        uaccess.put(pml4, old, asp, 8)
        uaccess.put(pml4, old + 8, if asz == 0 { SS_DISABLE } else if on { SS_ONSTACK } else { 0 }, 4)
        uaccess.put(pml4, old + 16, asz, 8)
    }
    sched.set_self_altstack(nsp, nsz)
    0
}

/// siginfo's code, pid, user id and status for an origin word.
fn origin(info: u64) -> (u64, u64, u64, u64) {
    let (kind, pid, status, uid) = sched.info_parts(info)
    if kind == sched.I_TKILL {
        return (SI_TKILL, pid, uid, 0)
    }
    if kind == sched.I_KERNEL {
        return (SI_KERNEL, 0, 0, 0)
    }
    if kind == sched.I_CHLD_EXITED {
        return (CLD_EXITED, pid, uid, status)
    }
    if kind == sched.I_CHLD_KILLED {
        return (CLD_KILLED, pid, uid, status)
    }
    if kind == sched.I_CHLD_STOPPED {
        return (CLD_STOPPED, pid, uid, status) // px48: si_status the stop's signal
    }
    if kind == sched.I_CHLD_CONTINUED {
        return (CLD_CONTINUED, pid, uid, status) // px48: si_status SIGCONT
    }
    (SI_USER, pid, uid, 0)
}

/// Act on signal `sig` (origin `info`), taken by the running thread
/// about to resume the frame `f`: the frame to resume.
fn act(f: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) -> u64 {
    let a = area()
    let w = SIG_WORDS + (sig - 1) * 4
    var h = aget(a, w)
    if sig == SIGKILL || sig == SIGSTOP {
        h = SIG_DFL
    }
    if h == SIG_IGN {
        return f
    }
    if h == SIG_DFL {
        if sched.self_pid() == 1 {
            return f
        }
        if sig == SIGCHLD || sig == SIGURG || sig == SIGWINCH || sig == SIGCONT {
            return f
        }
        if sig == SIGSTOP || sig == SIGTSTP || sig == SIGTTIN || sig == SIGTTOU {
            // px48: a stop that was blocked when it was sent, taken now: the
            // process stops (or, orphaned, does not), and `on_return`
            // parks this thread before it takes anything more.
            sched.stop_self(sig)
            return f
        }
        let (k, _p, _s, _u) = sched.info_parts(info)
        return die(f, sig, if k == sched.I_PIPE { ", a write to a pipe with no reader" } else if k == sched.I_CONN { ", a write to a connection that is over" } else { "" })
    }
    let (susp, was) = sched.self_suspended() // px48: rt_sigsuspend's mask goes back at the handler's end
    settle_wait(f, aget(a, w + 1) & SA_RESTART != 0)
    let (code, pid, uid, status) = origin(info)
    if !enter(f, sig, code, pid, uid, status, false, 0, 0, 0, if susp { was } else { sched.self_sigmask() }) {
        return die(f, SIGSEGV, ", no room on its stack for a signal's frame")
    }
    f
}

/// The frame `f` is about to be resumed (kernel/interrupts, for every
/// vector): when it returns to ring 3 and the running thread has a
/// signal to take, take every one; the frame to resume, which is
/// another thread's when a signal ended this one's process.
pub fn on_return(f: u64) -> u64 {
    var g = f
    var guard: u64 = 0
    while guard < 256 {
        if word(g, W_CS) & 3 != 3 {
            return g
        }
        if sched.self_parks() {
            // px48: its process is stopped: it runs nothing, and takes
            // nothing, until continued (or killed: SIGKILL readies it).
            g = sched.park_current(g)
            guard += 1
            continue
        }
        if !sched.signal_waits() {
            let (susp, _was) = sched.self_suspended()
            if susp {
                // px49 (pax#75): out of rt_sigsuspend with no handler run
                // (a stop and a continue, or another thread took the
                // signal): it waits again.
                g = sched.resuspend(g)
                guard += 1
                continue
            }
            return g
        }
        let (sig, info) = sched.take_signal()
        if sig == 0 {
            return g
        }
        g = act(g, sig, info)
        guard += 1
    }
    g
}

/// `rt_sigreturn()` from the frame `f` (a system call's): the frame as
/// the ucontext at its RSP says, or the next thread's when that cannot
/// be honoured and the process ends with SIGSEGV.
pub fn sigreturn(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let pml4 = paging.live()
    let uc = word(f, W_RSP)
    if !uaccess.readable(pml4, uc, UC_BYTES) {
        return die(f, SIGSEGV, ", rt_sigreturn with no frame to read")
    }
    let m = uc + UC_MCONTEXT
    let rip = uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 128)
    let rsp = uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 120)
    if rip >= paging.USER_TOP || rsp >= paging.USER_TOP {
        return die(f, SIGSEGV, ", rt_sigreturn to an address outside the user half")
    }
    let fp = uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 184)
    let fb = fpu.frame_bytes()
    if fp != 0 && fb != 0 {
        let s = sched.self_slot()
        let dst = fpu.area(s)
        if !uaccess.copy_in(pml4, dst, fp, fb) || !fpu.adopt(s) {
            fpu.exec(s)
            return die(f, SIGSEGV, ", rt_sigreturn with an extended state that cannot be loaded")
        }
    }
    put_word(f, W_R8, uaccess.get64(pml4, m))
    put_word(f, W_R9, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 8))
    put_word(f, W_R10, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 16))
    put_word(f, W_R11, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 24))
    put_word(f, W_R12, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 32))
    put_word(f, W_R13, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 40))
    put_word(f, W_R14, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 48))
    put_word(f, W_R15, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 56))
    put_word(f, W_RDI, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 64))
    put_word(f, W_RSI, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 72))
    put_word(f, W_RBP, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 80))
    put_word(f, W_RBX, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 88))
    put_word(f, W_RDX, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 96))
    put_word(f, W_RAX, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 104))
    put_word(f, W_RCX, uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 112))
    put_word(f, W_RSP, rsp)
    put_word(f, W_RIP, rip)
    put_word(f, W_RFLAGS, (uaccess.get64(pml4, m + 136) & FLAGS_USER) | FLAGS_FIXED)
    put_word(f, W_CS, USER_CS)
    put_word(f, W_SS, USER_SS)
    put_word(f, W_VECTOR, SIGNAL_VECTOR)
    let mask = uaccess.get64(pml4, uc + UC_SIGMASK) & !UNBLOCKABLE
    sched.set_self_sigmask(mask)
    sched.masks_changed()
    f
}

/// An exception in ring 3 (kernel/user's `kill`, vector `v`, frame
/// `f`): when the thread has a handler for its signal and does not
/// block it, the handler runs with the fault's siginfo and `f` is
/// returned pointing at it; otherwise 0, and kernel/user ends the
/// process as before px26 (Linux too ends a process whose fault's
/// signal is blocked or ignored: measured). px62: `bus`, a page fault
/// on a mapping's page past its file's end (kernel/vm), is SIGBUS with
/// BUS_ADRERR and the address, as Linux gives it (measured).
pub fn fault(f: u64, v: u64, bus: bool) -> u64 {
    let sig = if bus { SIGBUS } else { process.fault_signal(v) }
    let a = area()
    let w = SIG_WORDS + (sig - 1) * 4
    let h = aget(a, w)
    if h == SIG_DFL || h == SIG_IGN || sched.self_sigmask() & bit(sig) != 0 {
        return 0
    }
    var code = SI_KERNEL
    var addr: u64 = 0
    let err = word(f, W_ERROR)
    if v == 14 {
        // # Safety: cpu.S's MOV from CR2; this page fault set it.
        addr = unsafe { pax_cr2() }
        // Nothing at the address is SEGV_MAPERR; a page that is there, or
        // reserved (PROT_NONE), and refuses the access is SEGV_ACCERR.
        code = if err & 1 != 0 || vm.reserved(area(), addr) { SEGV_ACCERR } else { SEGV_MAPERR }
        if bus {
            code = BUS_ADRERR
        }
    } else if v == 0 {
        code = FPE_INTDIV
        addr = word(f, W_RIP)
    } else if v == 6 {
        code = ILL_ILLOPN
        addr = word(f, W_RIP)
    } else if v == 16 || v == 19 {
        code = FPE_FLTINV
        addr = word(f, W_RIP)
    }
    let _w = sched.self_wait()
    if !enter(f, sig, code, 0, 0, 0, true, addr, v, err, sched.self_sigmask()) {
        return 0
    }
    f
}
